 to the prediction task.\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area4'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Slope_Elevation_Ratio\n# Combines slope and elevation in a ratio to capture steepness relative to height. Areas with steep slopes at higher elevations could have unique vegetation and soil characteristics.\n# Usefulness: This feature could help uncover specific terrain types that correlate with forest cover types.\ndf['Slope_Elevation_Ratio'] = df['Slope'] / (df['Elevation'] + 1)  # Adding 1 to avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Hillshade_Range\n# Calculates the range (difference) between the highest and lowest hillshade values (9am, Noon, 3pm) to capture how much direct sunlight changes over the day.\n# Usefulness: Shadow variations can indicate terrain features that affect vegetation and forest cover types.\ndf['Hillshade_Range'] = df[['Hillshade_9am', 'Hillshade_Noon', 'Hillshade_3pm']].max(axis=1) - df[['Hillshade_9am', 'Hillshade_Noon', 'Hillshade_3pm']].min(axis=1)\nend\n\npython\n# Distance_Hydrology_Product\n# Multiplies the horizontal and vertical distances to hydrology to capture a combined relationship between proximity and elevation difference from water sources.\n# Usefulness: Areas with a higher product could indicate far-away or topographically distant locations from water sources, which may have different vegetation patterns.\ndf['Distance_Hydrology_Product'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] * df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type1 is dropped\n# Soil_Type1 has zero variance (all values are 0), meaning it does not contribute any information to differentiate between cover types.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type1'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type5 is dropped\n# Soil_Type5 also has zero variance (all values are 0), and therefore, it does not provide useful information for predictions.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type5'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Wilderness_Area2 is dropped\n# Wilderness_Area2 has a very low mean (Mean = 0.0598) and appears to represent a narrowly-specific region. Removing it can prevent overfitting to rare features.\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area2'], inplace=True)\nend", "python\n# Feature name: Elevation_Rank\n# Feature description: Rank the elevation values within the dataset.\n# Usefulness: Elevation impacts vegetation and forest cover type significantly. Using rank instead of raw elevation can reduce sensitivity to extreme values and help capture relative positioning in the landscape more intuitively.\ndf['Elevation_Rank'] = df['Elevation'].rank(method=average)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Wilderness_Area_Count\n# Feature description: Count the number of wilderness area indicators that are equal to 1 for each cell.\n# Usefulness: Cells in multiple wilderness areas may exhibit unique forest cover characteristics, and this aggregation helps understand the collective impact of wilderness zones on the target variable.\ndf['Wilderness_Area_Count'] = df[['Wilderness_Area1', 'Wilderness_Area2', 'Wilderness_Area3', 'Wilderness_Area4']].sum(axis=1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Slope_To_Hydrology_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio between slope and horizontal distance to hydrology.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines terrain steepness with proximity to water sources, capturing how hydrology interacts with the terrain, which could differentiate vegetation and soil stability patterns influencing forest cover type.\ndf['Slope_To_Hydrology_Ratio'] = df['Slope'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + 1)  # Adding 1 to prevent division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type37 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type37'], inplace=True)\n# Soil_Type37 has zero variance (all entries are 0), making it redundant and non-predictive for the task at hand. Dropping it streamlines the dataset.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Aspect is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Aspect'], inplace=True)\n# Aspect has circular relationships (0 degrees is close to 360 degrees), which are difficult for typical models to interpret directly. Transformations like sine and cosine can better capture its variation if necessary, making the raw column less useful.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Wilderness_Area4 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area4'], inplace=True)\n# Wilderness_Area4 has extremely low variance (mean = 0.0054), indicating it contributes minimal information for predicting the target variable. Removing it avoids noise in the dataset.\nend", "### Feature Engineering: Next Three Steps\n\npython\n# Feature name: Elevation_Normalized\n# Feature description: Normalize the Elevation feature using min-max scaling.\ndf['Elevation_Normalized'] = (df['Elevation'] - df['Elevation'].min()) / (df['Elevation'].max() - df['Elevation'].min())\n# Usefulness: By normalizing Elevation, the model can better handle relative differences in heights across the dataset without being skewed by the large range of raw values. Elevation is strongly tied to vegetation and forest type, so relative scaling helps models train more effectively.\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Hydrology_Total_Gradient\n# Feature description: Combine Horizontal and Vertical Distances to Hydrology into a normalized gradient (vertical change per unit horizontal distance).\ndf['Hydrology_Total_Gradient'] = df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + 1)  # Avoid division by zero\n# Usefulness: This captures terrain steepness in relation to water features, which could influence water flow and vegetation. Steeper or flat gradients may impact forest cover differently.\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Hillshade_Sum\n# Feature description: Compute the sum of Hillshade values at 9am, noon, and 3pm.\ndf['Hillshade_Sum'] = df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']\n# Usefulness: Summing hillshade values provides a cumulative measure of overall sunlight availability for a given area, which directly impacts vegetation growth and forest cover.\nend\n\n### Dropping Columns for Better Feature Selection\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Wilderness_Area4 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area4'], inplace=True)\n# Wilderness_Area4 has a very low mean (Mean = 0.0054) and extremely low occurrence. It contributes little information and may increase noise instead of improving predictions.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type9 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type9'], inplace=True)\n# Soil_Type9 has a very low mean (Mean = 0.0022) and is effectively underrepresented in the dataset, adding negligible predictive contribution to forest cover classification.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)\n# Soil_Type36 is nearly absent from the dataset (Mean = 0.0001), making it irrelevant for model training as it does not meaningfully differentiate between the forest cover types.\nend", "python\n# Feature name: Elevation_Binned\n# Feature description: Bin the Elevation feature into discrete categories using fixed intervals (e.g., 200-meter increments).\ndf['Elevation_Binned'] = pd.cut(df['Elevation'], bins=10, labels=False)\n# Usefulness: Elevation is highly correlated with vegetation, and binning this feature could help the model by grouping similar elevation ranges together, emphasizing non-linear elevation effects on forest type.\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Hydrology_Euclidean_Distance\n# Feature description: Calculate the Euclidean distance to hydrology using the vertical and horizontal distances.\ndf['Hydrology_Euclidean_Distance'] = ((df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology']2) + (df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']2))0.5\n# Usefulness: This feature provides a unified measure of proximity to water sources, combining both horizontal and vertical dimensions, which can better indicate vegetation patterns that depend on water availability.\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Hillshade_Mean\n# Feature description: Calculate the average hillshade across 9am, noon, and 3pm.\ndf['Hillshade_Mean'] = df[['Hillshade_9am', 'Hillshade_Noon', 'Hillshade_3pm']].mean(axis=1)\n# Usefulness: The average hillshade value captures the overall level of sunlight exposure throughout the day, which may be more informative than individual values for vegetation growth patterns.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type15 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type15'], inplace=True)\n# Soil_Type15 has zero variance (all values are 0), providing no useful information for predicting forest cover type, and can be safely removed.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type8 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\n# Soil_Type8 has a near-zero mean (Mean = 0.0003) and extremely low variance, indicating minimal importance for predictions. It adds noise rather than value.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Wilderness_Area4 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area4'], inplace=True)\n# Wilderness_Area4 has very low variance (Mean = 0.0054), providing negligible differentiation for forest cover types, and can be removed to reduce feature clutter.\nend"]###,
